mp":"2026-11-26T11:37:04.979Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"diff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 6a9e1836..c77352ce 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -1042,7 +1042,7 @@ Pass `{session}` as an entire argv element, never inside shell code. Captures fr\n \n Run `pose delegate ls [--json]` to inspect interruptions, whether a partial draft exists, reset instants and remaining wait. `pose doctor` warns about interrupted attempt endpoints. Runs finish completed, failed or interrupted; legacy stale records remain readable. Undeclared interruptions are unknown and not resumable. An interrupted draft is partial evidence, never a rejection or a review.\n \n-Run `pose delegate resume <run>` (`--dry-run` previews it; repeating it returns the recorded continuation) to continue with the same agent, captured session, brief digest, sealed commit and reconstructed checkout digest. Resume uses the original attempt and does not spend another attempt quota. The default maximum is three resumes and 60 minutes of accumulated wall-clock; configure `resume.max_resumes`/`max_wall_time` to change these bounds. Policy changes, changed inputs, lost or rewritten predecessors, terminal predecessors and spent budgets refuse. An immutable predecessor claim permits only one continuation; repeated requests return the recorded result or expose its pending reservation without launching another process. A lost host before a result is published remains explicit incomplete evidence; this is not automatic recovery without a captured interrupted record.\n+Run `pose delegate resume <run>` (`--dry-run` previews it; repeating it returns the recorded continuation) to continue with the same agent, captured session, brief digest, sealed commit and reconstructed checkout digest. Every run of an attempt recreates its sealed checkout at the same path under the dispatcher's temporary directory, so the resumed session finds the checkout it worked in; resume from a shell with the same `TMPDIR`. When that directory is still there although the last run cleaned up (another process holds the path), resume and handoff refuse before they claim the run: when no run of the attempt is live, remove the directory, then resume. A run whose cleanup did not complete is not resumed at all. Resume uses the original attempt and does not spend another attempt quota. The default maximum is three resumes and 60 minutes of accumulated wall-clock; configure `resume.max_resumes`/`max_wall_time` to change these bounds. Policy changes, changed inputs, lost or rewritten predecessors, terminal predecessors and spent budgets refuse. An immutable predecessor claim permits only one continuation; repeated requests return the recorded result or expose its pending reservation without launching another process. A lost host before a result is published remains explicit incomplete evidence; this is not automatic recovery without a captured interrupted record.\n \n Use `--wait-until-reset[=<duration>]` to wait once for a recorded reset, plus at most one second of jitter, then revalidate and resume. Default and maximum wait are 24 hours; a missing or more distant reset refuses. No vendor polling or automatic fallback occurs. To choose another agent after a pending usage limit, run it explicitly with `--agent <name>`; this creates a new attempt with `limit-of:<run>` and discloses both runs. Insights reports resumes separately from attempts and reruns; declared draft approval rates use completed unsuperseded drafts only.\n \n@@ -1071,10 +1071,12 @@ Preview `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <name> [--ttl <duration>]`, then\n \n Attach `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` to a complete `pose review attest` request. The native issuer derives authority principal/execution bindings from the run and signs the exact reference. Criteria, tools, evidence and all ordinary preflight checks still apply. A rejecting or ambiguous draft cannot support an approval; a run-backed attestation cannot predate its completed run or signed proof. Adjudication/smoke run proofs bind their kinds but cannot be used as review attestations. Automatic validation/application of complete typed conclusions belongs to the capability, not to run signing.\n \n-**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/10` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/10` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n+**Conclusions of delegated reviews.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <name>] [--apply] [--json]` records the conclusion of a completed review run: the run's final message carries its typed conclusion in the pose-draft block — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` as `<class>:<ref>`, `rationale`, and `mappings` where the plan asks for structural mappings) and `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), the brief of template `review-brief/11` (the text a template version renders is frozen by a golden digest, so a change to it takes a new version) asks for them and states the rules the engine checks before it records one (a judgment criterion passed carries a rationale, its evidence is a ref the bundle sealed, a finding criterion names a finding of the draft, structural facts are answered as `mappings`; a draft that breaks one is refused whole; a round after the first also names the findings it accounts for by the identifiers the brief lists, with their round, never a bare F1) — and the engine signs the run's proof and the attestation with the native issuer, `--issuer` or `conclusions.issuer` in `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (the key stays outside the project), and records it through the same domain writer and preflight as `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. A follow-up finding with `action`, `owner` and `review_by` becomes an accepted risk when its severity is one the sealed review policy accepts (low by default); any other finding stays open. A rejecting conclusion is recorded too and blocks the scope until the bundle changes. A run that is not a completed review, is superseded, captured no provenance, has an incomplete cleanup or record, carries no valid draft for its round, or whose bundle no longer governs, records no attestation: its conclusion record, in the `review-conclusions` directory of `.pose`, keeps the reasons. A conclusion that requests changes or rejects is recorded even while the working tree already holds edits of the sealed subject, since it is a fact about the bundle it reviewed; an approval refused only because the working tree no longer holds its bundle's subject is reported without a record, and the same run concludes once the tree matches. A run concludes once: a lock the system releases when the process ends is held from the first read to the last write, concluding it again returns its record, and a reservation an interrupted conclusion left is completed from the attestation it recorded only if that attestation verifies as the run's conclusion, otherwise the run is concluded in its place. Whoever signs it, an attestation of a run briefed with `review-brief/3` to `/11` states that run's conclusion: criteria, tools or findings its draft did not declare are refused. With review policy `delegated_review: true` and `conclusions.issuer` set, `pose delegate review` records the conclusion the moment the run completes; otherwise nothing is automatic. An adjudication or a smoke run concludes the same way, under the same checks, lock and refusals, and never as an approval. The brief of template `adjudication-brief/2` lists the questions the scope's spec records — each decision `D<n>` and requirement `R<n>` — and the pose-draft block answers each in `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); the verdict is recorded as an adjudication record of the spec in the `adjudications` directory of `.pose`, and `lint-spec` fails, and `close` refuses, on a question the current bundle's adjudication says does not hold and warns on one it cannot determine or on a record that does not verify. The brief of template `smoke-brief/2` asks for `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), one per delivery target; the observations are recorded as validation results of evidence class `smoke`, one required check per target that passes when it was observed and is evidence of that target of that spec only, its head, time and provenance taken from the run and the current index, in the `results/smoke` directory of `.pose`, which `surface-check` reads beside the results path, so a delivery profile may demand `smoke`. A verdict that does not answer exactly what its brief asked records nothing; every record is verified on read — its run's proof verifies and it is what the run's draft concludes — and one that does not yields nothing. Runs briefed with `adjudication-brief/1` and `smoke-brief/1` keep verifying with their template.\n \n **Review readiness before the seal.** A new seal of a spec scope refuses what `pose close` and the conclusion preflight would otherwise refuse only after a full review round: a change to a delivery root with no delivery declaration (`undeclared-delivery`) or to a governed path the artifacts do not declare (`undeclared`), read from the same index `pose close` reads, and, when a required criterion asks for structural mappings, a decision or assumption that reaches no requirement or constraint (give it a `- Basis:` line). A bundle already sealed is returned as it was. `pose review draft-check [<bundle-id|run-id>] --draft <file|-> [--json]` reports every reason the conclusion step would refuse a draft (an unreadable block, a round it does not account for, a criterion, mapping, finding, evidence or tool the attestation preflight refuses) and writes nothing; inside a delegated review the run provides the bundle and the round through `POSE_REVIEW_DRAFT_CONTEXT` and names its own engine in `POSE_REVIEW_CLI`, and `-` reads the draft from standard input, so it works in a read-only sandbox. The brief of template `review-brief/8` asks the reviewer to run it before finishing; `review-brief/9` also has every planned `pose` command run as `\"$POSE_REVIEW_CLI\"`, because the `pose` the reviewer's shell resolves may be an older engine. `pose delegate` names an oversized note file with its size and the limit, and a completed run that nothing concluded automatically prints the `pose review conclude <run>` command that records it.\n \n+**Accepted limits.** A spec may declare the residual limits of its guarantees under `### Accepted limits`, one `- L<N>: <assumption>. Accepted because <reason>.` per limit, whose wrapped lines are indented under it; the section holds only limits, and any other line in it is reported and leaves the limit it belongs to unsealed (HTML comments are not read); `lint-spec --ready-check` refuses a limit that does not say why it is accepted, and, when an ADR cites the spec, one the ADR does not name. The review plan and the bundle seal the limits (`accepted_limits`, part of the plan digest; in a scope with several specs each id is qualified as `<spec>#L<N>`), and the brief of template `review-brief/11` lists them: a finding that falls inside one is a follow-up with `limit` set to its id, recorded in the attestation with disposition `within-limit`, which does not block an approval whatever its severity and is counted apart in the governance outcomes; a limit the bundle did not seal, or a `within-limit` finding without a reason, is refused. A reviewer who finds a limit unjustified reports a finding that disputes it, which blocks as any finding does. A review converges by classifying what was declared out of scope, not by a cap on rounds.\n+\n **Runs that leave work behind.** On Linux the dispatch runs the adapter under a review run shim, the same executable re-entered, which is the subreaper of the run's subtree: after the adapter exits, on a timeout, and when its dispatcher ends, it ends and reaps every process left in that subtree, even one that started its own session and cleared its environment, and reports the ended pids through a pipe the reviewer does not inherit; the dispatch lists them in the run's `safety.limitations` before the cleanup inventory. An embedder that does not enable the shim falls back to ending the processes whose environment names the run's sealed checkout (`POSE_SEALED_CHECKOUT`); on other Unix platforms only the process group is ended, and on Windows the adapter runs in a kill-on-close job object that the dispatch terminates after the run, on a timeout and, through the job's limit, when the dispatcher ends. The brief of template `review-brief/10` asks the reviewer to let its commands finish. The dispatch marks an attempt active before it publishes its reservation, so a dispatch that ends before recording its run always leaves a stalled attempt, which `pose delegate ls` lists with its cause, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt>` releases it once its owner is proven gone, recording a failed run and its receipt; one release runs at a time, under a lock the system releases with its holder, and a release completes with any run the attempt already recorded instead of writing another. A reservation an earlier engine left without a marker records no owner: `pose delegate ls` lists it once it is ten minutes old, and `pose delegate cancel <attempt> --confirm-abandoned` releases it on the operator's confirmation that no dispatch of it runs.\n \n **Delegated-review requests.** `pose review request <scope|bundle> --kind delegated-review [--purpose review|adjudicate|smoke] [--agents <name,...>] [--note-file <path>] [--apply]` opens an action request of kind `delegated-review` that binds the project, the worktree, the scope, the sealed bundle, the brief, the notes, the policies, the accepted agents and the destination of the conclusion (an attestation, an adjudication record or smoke evidence). No actor answers it: `pose action fulfil <act-id> --run <run-id> --runner agent:<id> --idempotency-key <key> --expected-revision <n> [--channel cli|ci|conductor] [--envelope <file>] [--apply]`, or the MCP tool `pose_action_fulfil`, submits a completed run, and the engine records the fulfilment only when the run is of the request's brief and bundle, of an accepted agent, completed with its containment, its signed proof verifies, its attempt ledger is intact and the engine recorded its conclusion at the request's destination; the fulfilment is verified again on every read. `pose delegate review <scope> --request <act-id>` runs the request's brief locally and submits the run once concluded. A rejecting run fulfils the request without approving anything; a superseded bundle or changed policy invalidates it, even once fulfilled, and it is asked again with `--supersedes <act-id>`, which retires it under the supersession authority rules (`--authority <json>`, `--role <role>`, `--requested-at` from the preview), or, when the plan now reserves the review for a person, makes the person's escalation its successor; a retry with the same key and run is a no-op. A person is asked by exception, as a decision request to the maintainer role: a plan that reserves the review for a person, a spent attempt budget, reviewers in conflict, and a `business_decision` a reviewer raised in its draft. A run's proof verifies in any checkout of the project: the worktree it ran in is recorded, not required of the reader, so a CI job or the Conductor fulfils the request with the same envelope.\ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 917602e3..283e2f6a 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -1039,7 +1039,7 @@ Passe `{session}` como argumento inteiro, nunca dentro de código shell. As capt\n \n Execute `pose delegate ls [--json]` para ver interrupções, se há draft parcial, reset e tempo restante. `pose doctor` avisa sobre endpoints interrompidos. Runs terminam completed, failed ou interrupted; registros stale legados continuam legíveis. Sem declarações, interrupções são unknown e não retomáveis. Um draft interrompido é evidência parcial, nunca rejeição ou review.\n \n-Execute `pose delegate resume <run>` (`--dry-run` mostra antes; repetir devolve a continuação registrada) para retomar com o mesmo agente, sessão capturada, digest do brief, commit selado e digest do checkout reconstruído. A continuação usa a tentativa original, sem consumir outra quota. O padrão permite três resumes e 60 minutos acumulados de wall-clock; altere com `resume.max_resumes`/`max_wall_time`. Mudanças de policy/inputs, predecessor perdido ou reescrito, predecessor terminal e orçamento esgotado impedem resume. Uma reserva imutável por predecessor permite apenas uma continuação; repetições retornam o resultado existente ou mostram a reserva pendente sem iniciar outro processo. Perder o host antes de publicar resultado permanece evidência incompleta explícita; não há recuperação automática sem um registro interrompido capturado.\n+Execute `pose delegate resume <run>` (`--dry-run` mostra antes; repetir devolve a continuação registrada) para retomar com o mesmo agente, sessão capturada, digest do brief, commit selado e digest do checkout reconstruído. Todas as runs de uma tentativa recriam o checkout selado no mesmo caminho, sob o diretório temporário de quem despacha, para que a sessão retomada encontre o checkout em que trabalhava; retome de um shell com o mesmo `TMPDIR`. Se esse diretório ainda existir embora a última run tenha limpado (outro processo ocupa o caminho), o resume e o handoff recusam antes de reservar a run: sem nenhuma run da tentativa viva, remova o diretório e retome. Uma run cuja limpeza não terminou não é retomada. A continuação usa a tentativa original, sem consumir outra quota. O padrão permite três resumes e 60 minutos acumulados de wall-clock; altere com `resume.max_resumes`/`max_wall_time`. Mudanças de policy/inputs, predecessor perdido ou reescrito, predecessor terminal e orçamento esgotado impedem resume. Uma reserva imutável por predecessor permite apenas uma continuação; repetições retornam o resultado existente ou mostram a reserva pendente sem iniciar outro processo. Perder o host antes de publicar resultado permanece evidência incompleta explícita; não há recuperação automática sem um registro interrompido capturado.\n \n Use `--wait-until-reset[=<duração>]` para esperar uma vez pelo reset registrado, mais até um segundo de jitter, e revalidar antes de retomar. O padrão e máximo são 24 horas; reset ausente ou além do limite impede a espera. Não há polling do fornecedor nem fallback automático. Para escolher outro agente após um limite pendente, rode-o explicitamente com `--agent <nome>`; ele cria outra tentativa com `limit-of:<run>` e mostra ambos os runs. Insights conta resumes separadamente de tentativas/reruns; taxas declaradas de aprovação usam somente drafts completed não superseded.\n \n@@ -1068,10 +1068,12 @@ Visualize `pose review run-sign <run-id> --issuer <nome> [--ttl <duração>]` e\n \n Acrescente `--run <run-id> --run-proof <proof-id> --sign <issuer>` a uma solicitação completa de `pose review attest`. O issuer deriva do run os vínculos de principal/execução e assina a referência exata. Critérios, tools, evidências e preflight continuam obrigatórios. Draft negativo ou ambíguo não apoia aprovação; a atestação não pode anteceder o término do run ou sua prova assinada. Provas de adjudication/smoke vinculam seus kinds e não servem como atestações de review. Validar/aplicar automaticamente conclusões completas e tipadas pertence à capability, não à assinatura do run.\n \n-**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/10` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveniência, tem limpeza ou registro incompletos, não traz draft válido para sua rodada, ou cujo bundle não governa mais, não registra atestação: seu registro de conclusão, no diretório `review-conclusions` de `.pose`, guarda os motivos. Um run conclui uma vez: um lock que o sistema libera quando o processo termina é mantido da primeira leitura à última escrita, concluí-lo de novo devolve seu registro, e uma reserva deixada por uma conclusão interrompida só é completada pela atestação que ela registrou se essa atestação verificar como a conclusão do run; caso contrário o run é concluído no lugar dela. Quem quer que a assine, uma atestação de um run com brief `review-brief/3` a `/10` declara a conclusão daquele run: critérios, ferramentas ou findings que o draft não declarou são recusados. Com `delegated_review: true` na policy de review e `conclusions.issuer` definido, `pose delegate review` registra a conclusão no momento em que o run termina; caso contrário nada é automático. Um run de adjudicação ou de smoke conclui do mesmo jeito, sob os mesmos checks, lock e recusas, e nunca como aprovação. O brief do template `adjudication-brief/2` lista as questões que a spec do escopo registra — cada decisão `D<n>` e requisito `R<n>` — e o bloco pose-draft responde cada uma em `questions` (`id`, `verdict` holds|does-not-hold|cannot-determine, `evidence`, `rationale`); o veredito é registrado como registro de adjudicação da spec no diretório `adjudications` de `.pose`, e o `lint-spec` reprova, e o `close` recusa, uma questão que a adjudicação do bundle atual diz não se sustentar e avisa sobre uma que ela não pôde determinar ou sobre um registro que não verifica. O brief do template `smoke-brief/2` pede `surfaces` (`ref`, `outcome` observed|not-observed|not-reachable, `commands`, `observed`), uma por delivery target; as observações são registradas como resultados de validação da classe de evidência `smoke`, um check obrigatório por alvo que passa quando foi observado e é evidência só daquele alvo daquela spec, com head, data e proveniência tirados do run e do índice atual, no diretório `results/smoke` de `.pose`, que o `surface-check` lê ao lado do results path, de modo que um delivery profile pode exigir `smoke`. Um veredito que não responde exatamente o que seu brief pediu não registra nada; todo registro é verificado na leitura — a prova do run verifica e ele é o que o draft do run conclui — e um que não verifica não produz nada. Runs com brief `adjudication-brief/1` e `smoke-brief/1` continuam verificando com o próprio template.\n+**Conclusões de reviews delegadas.** `pose review conclude <run> [--issuer <nome>] [--apply] [--json]` registra a conclusão de um run de review concluído: a mensagem final do run traz a conclusão tipada no bloco pose-draft — `criteria` (`id`, `disposition` passed|finding|not-applicable, `evidence` como `<classe>:<ref>`, `rationale` e, quando o plano pede mapeamentos estruturais, `mappings`) e `tools` (`id`, `component`, `disposition`, `evidence`, `rationale`), que o brief do template `review-brief/11` (o texto que uma versão de template renderiza é congelado por um digest de referência, então mudá-lo exige uma nova versão) pede e para o qual declara as regras que o motor confere antes de registrar (um critério de julgamento aprovado traz uma justificativa, sua evidência é uma ref que o bundle selou, um critério com finding nomeia um finding do draft, fatos estruturais são respondidos como `mappings`; um draft que quebra uma delas é recusado inteiro; uma rodada depois da primeira também nomeia os findings pelos quais responde com os identificadores que o brief lista, com a rodada, nunca um F1 solto) — e o motor assina a prova do run e a atestação com o issuer nativo, `--issuer` ou `conclusions.issuer` em `.pose/policy/delegation.json` (a chave fica fora do projeto), e a registra pelo mesmo writer de domínio e preflight de `pose review attest --run --run-proof --sign`. Um finding de follow-up com `action`, `owner` e `review_by` vira um risco aceito quando sua severidade é uma que a política de review selada aceita (low por padrão); qualquer outro finding fica aberto. Uma conclusão que rejeita também é registrada e bloqueia o escopo até o bundle mudar. Um run que não é uma review concluída, foi substituído, não capturou proveni�","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"persistedOutputPath":"/home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-874c23d9d3-workt